==Conduct==
More than 6,780 security personnel, including units of the [[ECOWAS]] Stabilisation Force, were deployed to maintain order during and after the election.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.rfi.fr/en/africa/20251126-guinea-bissau-military-claims-to-have-seized-power-after-disputed-vote |title=Guinea-Bissau military claims to have seized power after disputed vote |work=RFI |date=26 November 2025}}</ref> More than 200 observers monitored the conduct of the election, including representatives of ECOWAS, the [[African Union]] and the [[Community of Portuguese Language Countries]].<ref name="claim"/> On 25 November, the head of the ECOWAS observer mission, [[Issifu Baba Braimah Kamara]], said the election had been conducted peacefully.<ref name="f24"/>

==Results==
{{Main|2025 Guinea-Bissau coup d'état}}
Both Embaló and [[Fernando Dias]] claimed victory in the presidential election on 24 November, despite official results not having been released yet.
<ref name="claim">{{cite news |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/11/25/guinea-bissau-rivals-embalo-dias-claim-win-in-presidential-election|title=Guinea-Bissau rivals Embalo, Dias claim win in presidential election |work=Al Jazeera |date=25 November 2025}}</ref> A member of the West African observation team said Embaló had lost the election.<ref>https://www.nytimes.com/2025/11/27/world/africa/guinea-bissau-coup.html</ref>

On 26 November, a day before the release of official results, gunfire was heard outside the presidential palace in [[Bissau]] and the National Electoral Commission, with Embaló saying that he had been arrested as part of a [[2025 Guinea-Bissau coup d'état|coup d'état]] carried out by Head of the Military Office of the Presidency, Brigadier General [[Dinis Incanha]]. The [[Revolutionary Armed Forces of the People (Guinea-Bissau)|military]] subsequently declared "total control" over the country and established the [[High Military Command for the Restoration of National Security and Public Order]].<ref name="f24">{{cite news |url=https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20251126-gunfire-heard-near-guinea-bissau-presidential-palace-provisional-election-results-expected |title=Gunfire heard near Guinea-Bissau presidential palace as provisional election results expected |work=France 24 |date=26 November 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theafricareport.com/400185/coup-in-guinea-bissau-embalo-arrested/ |title=Coup in Guinea-Bissau. Embaló arrested |work=The Africa Report |date=26 November 2025}}</ref> Dias was also initially reported to have been arrested, but later released a video saying that he had escaped and vowed to resist the coup. He was later given asylum at the Nigerian embassy. On 27 November, the High Military Command proclaimed army chief of staff General [[Horta Inta-A Na Man]] as head of a military government that would oversee a one-year transition period.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20251127-general-horta-nta-sworn-in-as-guinea-bissau-transitional-leader-following-coup |title=General Horta N'Tam sworn in as Guinea-Bissau transitional leader following coup |work=France 24 |date=27 November 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://apnews.com/article/guineabissau-coup-ecowas-talks-0b4b745cea6c30f97e0c34258fbd4a6c |title=A West African bloc delegation says talks with Guinea-Bissau coup leaders will continue |work=AP News |date=2 December 2025}}</ref> Later that day, Embalo was released and went into exile in Senegal following negotiations by the Senegalese government.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/11/27/guinea-bissaus-deposed-president-embalo-arrives-in-senegal-after-coup |title=Guinea-Bissau’s deposed President Embalo arrives in Senegal after coup |work=Al Jazeera |date=27 November 2025}}</ref> Embalo was subsequently accused by several opposition and international figures, including former Nigerian president and election monitor [[Goodluck Jonathan]], of staging the coup to avoid losing the election.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c1m8nm22785o |title=Was it coup or was it a 'sham'? Behind Guinea-Bissau's military takeover |work=BBC |date=2 December 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://apnews.com/article/guinea-bissau-embalo-military-takeover-9e7cfebdbd32430092d97cc291184dbc |title=Guinea-Bissau's latest coup sparks allegations of a staged takeover. Here's what to know |work=AP News |date=2 December 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://apnews.com/article/guinea-bissau-presidential-palace-unrest-election-2a1371eb1ceb25596b99c4cc4062bc95 |title=Soldiers in Guinea-Bissau appear on state television saying they have seized power in the country |work=AP News |date=26 November 2025}}</ref> 

On 29 November, protests were held in Bissau calling for the release of the election results, while three civil society groups called for a general strike and a civil disobedience campaign in order to restore "electoral truth".<ref name="sham"/>

On 2 December, the CNE said it was unable to publish the election results after armed men wearing [[Balaclava (clothing)|balaclava]]s confiscated computers from 45 staff and destroyed all tally sheets except for those from Bissau and the main computer server storing the results.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20251202-guinea-bissau-electoral-commission-says-armed-men-destroyed-election-ballots |title=Guinea-Bissau electoral commission says armed men destroyed election ballots |work=France 24 |date=2 December 2025}}</ref><ref name="sham">{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c1m8nm22785o |title=Was it coup or was it a 'sham'? Behind Guinea-Bissau's military takeover |work=BBC |date=2 December 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/12/2/guinea-bissaus-electoral-commission-says-coup-destroyed-election-results |title=Guinea-Bissau’s electoral commission says coup destroyed election results |work=Al Jazeera |date=2 December 2025}}</ref> However, some media outlets published the minutes with the election results:<ref name="Resultados">{{cite news |url=https://www.cmjornal.pt/mundo/detalhe/atas-confirmam-vitoria-de-fernando-dias-da-costa-nas-presidenciais-da-guine-bissau |title=Atas confirmam vitória de Fernando Dias da Costa nas presidenciais da Guiné-Bissau |work=Correio da Manhã |date=4 December 2025| access-date=6 December 2025}}</ref>

On 22 January 2026, the High Military Command issued a decree authorising [[2026 Guinea-Bissau general election|elections]] for the legislature and the presidency to be held on 6 December 2026, after it deemed that "all the conditions for organising free, fair and transparent elections have been met".<ref>{{Cite web |date=22 January 2026 |title=Guinea-Bissau set to hold general elections on December 6 |url=https://www.africanews.com/2026/01/21/guinea-bissau-set-to-hold-general-elections-on-december-6/ |access-date=22 January 2026 |website=Africanews |language=en }}</ref>

